---
/**
 * @file pages/privacy.astro
 * @description Privacy policy for akibh.dev AND blog.akibh.dev (one person,
 *              one policy — the blog links here; plan31).
 *
 * WHY THIS CONTENT (POC / rule for future edits): every statement below was
 * checked against the code, not copied from a template —
 *   contact form  → functions/api/contact.ts → mailer Worker (akibh-dev/mailer):
 *                   name/email/message are emailed, never stored; the IP is
 *                   used for an in-memory rate limit + Turnstile `remoteip`
 *   Turnstile     → Contact.astro (lazy, invisible widget)
 *   analytics     → Cloudflare Web Analytics beacon (edge-injected, cookieless)
 *   comments      → apps/blog Comments.astro (giscus → GitHub Discussions,
 *                   repo akibh-dev/notes), lazy iframe
 *   storage       → lib/theme.ts (`theme` in localStorage); fonts self-hosted
 *   search        → Pagefind, runs in the browser
 * If any of those changes (new analytics, a new form field, a newsletter…),
 * update this page and `updated` in the same commit.
 */
import LegalPage from "../layouts/LegalPage.astro";
import { portfolioData } from "../data/portfolio";

const { meta } = portfolioData;
const updated = "2026-10-23";
---

<LegalPage
  title="Privacy policy"
  description={`What data akibh.dev and blog.akibh.dev collect (very little), why, who processes it, and how to get it deleted.`}
  updated={updated}
>
  <p class="summary">
    <strong>Short version:</strong> I don't use ads, tracking cookies or profiling, and I don't sell or share your data.
    The contact form emails your message to me; blog comments live publicly on GitHub; Cloudflare hosts and protects
    both sites and counts page views without cookies.
  </p>

  <h2>Who I am</h2>
  <p>
    These sites — <a href="https://akibh.dev">akibh.dev</a> (portfolio) and{" "}
    <a href={meta.blogUrl}>blog.akibh.dev</a> (blog) — are run by me, {meta.name}, an individual based in{" "}
    {meta.location}. For anything about your data, email <a href={`mailto:${meta.email}`}>{meta.email}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The contact form (portfolio)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>What:</strong> the name, email address and message you type. There is also a hidden anti-spam field
      that people never fill in.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Why:</strong> so I can read and answer your message. That's the only use — no mailing list, no
      marketing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>How:</strong> the form is sent to a small Cloudflare Worker that checks it and forwards it as an email,
      through Cloudflare Email Routing, to my personal inbox (Gmail, by Google). The site keeps{" "}
      <strong>no database</strong> of messages.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your IP address</strong> is used for two things: a short rate limit (at most 10 messages a minute,
      held in memory for about a minute) and the bot check below. It isn't written into the email or stored.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>How long:</strong> the email stays in my inbox while we're in touch and for my records. Ask me and I'll
      delete it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Bot check: Cloudflare Turnstile</h3>
  <p>
    To stop spam, the form uses <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/application-services/products/turnstile/">Cloudflare Turnstile</a>,
    which runs invisibly in the background — there is no puzzle to solve. It only loads when you scroll near the
    form. It looks at technical signals from your browser (not the text you type) to tell people from bots, and
    Cloudflare says it doesn't use this data for advertising. Details:{" "}
    <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/turnstile-privacy-policy/">Turnstile privacy addendum</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Comments (blog)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Comments use <a href="https://giscus.app">giscus</a>, which stores them as{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/akibh-dev/notes/discussions">GitHub Discussions</a> in my public{" "}
      <code>akibh-dev/notes</code> repository.
    </li>
    <li>
      To comment or react you sign in with GitHub and allow the giscus app to post for you. Your comment is then{" "}
      <strong>public</strong> on GitHub under your GitHub account (username, avatar, text, time). I don't get your
      email or password.
    </li>
    <li>
      The comments box is loaded from giscus.app only when you scroll near it. If you sign in, giscus keeps a session
      token in your browser's storage for giscus.app.
    </li>
    <li>
      You can edit or delete your own comments on GitHub at any time. I may hide or delete comments that break the{" "}
      <a href="/terms/">terms</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Their policies: <a href="https://github.com/giscus/giscus/blob/main/PRIVACY-POLICY.md">giscus privacy policy</a>,{" "}
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub privacy statement</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Analytics</h2>
  <p>
    Both sites use <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/">Cloudflare Web Analytics</a> to count visits.
    It doesn't use cookies or local storage, doesn't fingerprint you, and only gives me totals — page views,
    referring sites, countries, browsers and page-load speed. I can't see who you are.
  </p>

  <h2>Hosting and security</h2>
  <p>
    Both sites are static pages served by <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/">Cloudflare</a> (Pages, CDN and
    security). Like any web host, Cloudflare processes your IP address and basic request details (browser, page,
    time) to deliver the page and block attacks. Its bot protection may set a strictly necessary security cookie
    such as <code>__cf_bm</code>. Details: <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare privacy policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Cookies and browser storage</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>I set no cookies.</strong> The only possible cookie is Cloudflare's security cookie above.</li>
    <li>
      Both sites remember your light/dark choice in your browser's local storage (key <code>theme</code>). It never
      leaves your device.
    </li>
    <li>Fonts are hosted on these sites themselves, so no font service sees your visit.</li>
    <li>
      Blog search runs entirely in your browser (<a href="https://pagefind.app">Pagefind</a>): what you search for is
      never sent anywhere.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Because nothing here tracks you, there's no cookie banner.</p>

  <h2>Who else sees data</h2>
  <p>
    Only the services named above, each for its stated job: Cloudflare (hosting, Turnstile, analytics, email
    delivery), Google (my email inbox), and GitHub with giscus (comments). Their servers may be outside your
    country, including in the United States. I don't sell, rent or share data with anyone else, and I'd only hand it
    over if the law required it.
  </p>

  <h2>Links to other sites</h2>
  <p>
    Links to GitHub, LinkedIn, X, Facebook, coding-contest sites and others take you to services with their own
    privacy policies. This policy stops at these two sites.
  </p>

  <h2>Your choices and rights</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Ask me what I have from you (in practice: your contact emails), to correct it, or to delete it — email{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${meta.email}`}>{meta.email}</a> and I'll reply within 30 days.
    </li>
    <li>Delete or edit your blog comments yourself on GitHub, or ask me to remove them.</li>
    <li>
      Depending on where you live (for example the EU/UK under the GDPR), you may also have the right to object, to
      restrict processing, and to complain to your data-protection authority. I rely on your request (when you write
      to me) and on my legitimate interest in running and protecting these sites.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>These sites aren't aimed at children under 13, and I don't knowingly collect their data.</p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If what the sites collect changes, this page changes with it and the date at the top is updated. The current
    version always lives at <a href="/privacy/">akibh.dev/privacy/</a>.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
